FRAMESPEED S1: views record where they drew; a frame whose every invalidate is invisible is skipped

View.draw records the view's footprint on the frame canvas (canvas translation and axis-aligned scale; a ViewGroup unions its children's), and invalidate / invalidateDrawable / onDescendantInvalidated add the old and the moved footprint to ViewRootImpl's damage (macapkConsumeDamage, read by the driver's S2 half). A view never drawn but shown, a scale or rotation change, a legacy Animation or any other schedule() makes the frame FULL; a view under zero scale or zero alpha (self or ancestor) changes nothing on screen. A draw-only frame with no damage is skipped. offsetLeftAndRight/offsetTopAndBottom and setLeftTopRightBottom now invalidate (AOSP repaints those moves; ViewOffsetHelper headers use them). TextureView.draw records its footprint too.

Measured on Instagram: a collapsed like-heart (scale 0) invalidated ~75x/s and every one repainted the window. MACAPK_DAMAGE_VALIDATE=1 checks both halves: the validator draws the frame fully, claims the damage (or one pixel for a frame the skip would drop) and logs one `under-damage box` line per frame with changed pixels outside the claim. Two tours over feed, search, Reels and profile: 0 wrong skips. Clipping draw-only frames to the damage (MACAPK_DAMAGE_CLIP=1) stays off: it cost the same as a full frame (draw 1.9 vs 1.7 ms, the tree walk dominates) and 11 of 581 clipped frames under-damaged. MACAPK_TRACE_DAMAGE=1 logs why frames went full and who claimed what. The validator now prints one summary line per frame (capped at 300 frames) instead of the first 64 rows of the process.

